{
  "dom_id": "step-q121189",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-1=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5},{x}_{6}$ are written in ascending order, what is the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{4}?$",
  "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={135}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{45}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{{135}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={15}^{∘}$ and $x={45}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={135}^{∘}$ and $x={165}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={255}^{∘}$ and $x={285}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5},{x}_{6}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={15}^{∘},{45}^{∘},{135}^{∘},{165}^{∘},{255}^{∘},{285}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}={45}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{4}={165}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{4}=45+165=210.\n$$",
